                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2757'                         EXADDR:
  2757'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2758'   32 2DA4'              	STA	SRNGF
  275B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  275C'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  275E'   CA 2787'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2761'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  2762'   CD 279C'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2765'   DA 2785'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2768'   22 2DA5'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  276B'   22 2DA7'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  276E'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  276F'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2771'   C2 277E'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2774'   13                    	INX	D
  2775'   CD 279C'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2778'   DA 2785'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  277B'   22 2DA7'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  277E'                         EXA_YS:
  277E'   EB                    	XCHG
  277F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2781'   32 2DA4'              	STA	SRNGF
  2784'   C9                    	RET
  2785'                         EXA_NO:
  2785'   EB                    	XCHG
  2786'   C9                    	RET
  2787'                         EXA_AL:
  2787'   23                    	INX	H
  2788'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2789'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  278C'   22 2DA5'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  278F'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2792'   22 2DA7'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2795'   E1                    	POP	H
  2796'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2798'   32 2DA4'              	STA	SRNGF
  279B'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  279C'                         EXNUM:
  279C'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  279D'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  279F'   D8                    	RC
  27A0'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27A2'   3F                    	CMC
  27A3'   D8                    	RC
  27A4'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  27A7'                         EXN_LP:
  27A7'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  27A8'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


  27AA'   DA 27C4'              	JC	EXN_DN
  27AD'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  27AF'   D2 27C4'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  27B2'   13                    	INX	D
  27B3'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  27B4'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  27B5'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  27B7'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  27B8'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  27B9'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  27BA'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  27BB'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  27BC'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  27BD'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  27BE'   D1                    	POP	D
  27BF'   09                    	DAD	B
  27C0'   C1                    	POP	B
  27C1'   C3 27A7'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  27C4'                         EXN_DN:
  27C4'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  27C5'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  27C6'                         EX_S:
  27C6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27C7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27C8'   CA 280A'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  27CB'   32 2DA9'              	STA	SDELIM
  27CE'   23                    	INX	H
  27CF'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  27D2'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  27D5'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  27D6'   CD 2810'              	CALL	SCOPY
  27D9'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  27DA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27DB'   CA 280A'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  27DE'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  27E1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27E3'   32 2D71'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  27E6'   11 2DBB'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  27E9'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  27EB'   CD 2810'              	CALL	SCOPY
  27EE'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  27EF'   32 2DBA'              	STA	SREPLN
  27F2'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27F3'   32 2DAA'              	STA	SGFLAG
  27F6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27F7'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  27F9'   C2 2802'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  27FC'   23                    	INX	H
  27FD'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  27FF'   32 2DAA'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2802'                         EX_SG:
  2802'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2803'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  2804'   C2 2591'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2807'   C3 2830'              	JMP	SUBGO
  280A'                         SUBNOP:
  280A'   21 2659'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  280D'   C3 259F'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2810'                         SCOPY:
  2810'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2812'                         SCP_LP:
  2812'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2813'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2814'   D2 282D'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2817'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2818'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2819'   CA 282D'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  281C'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  281D'   21 2DA9'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2820'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2821'   E1                    	POP	H
  2822'   CA 282C'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2825'   12                    	STAX	D
  2826'   13                    	INX	D
  2827'   23                    	INX	H
  2828'   04                    	INR	B
  2829'   C3 2812'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  282C'                         SCP_SK:
  282C'   23                    	INX	H
  282D'                         SCP_DN:
  282D'   AF                    	XRA	A
  282E'   12                    	STAX	D
  282F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2830'                         SUBGO:
  2830'   CD 122C'              	CALL	GZONE
  2833'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2836'   22 2DB8'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2839'   21 2DB5'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  283C'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  283F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2840'   32 2DAB'              	STA	SANY
  2843'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2846'   22 2DAD'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2849'   3A 2DA4'              	LDA	SRNGF
  284C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  284D'   CA 285F'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2850'   CD 211F'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2853'   DA 28DB'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2856'   2A 2DA5'              	LHLD	SRNG1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  2859'   CD 119A'              	CALL	GOLN
  285C'   C3 2865'              	JMP	SUBLP
  285F'                         SUB_1L:
  285F'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2862'   22 2DAF'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2865'                         SUBLP:
  2865'   CD 28E1'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2868'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2869'   CA 2877'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  286C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  286E'   32 2DAB'              	STA	SANY
  2871'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2874'   22 2DAD'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2877'                         SUB_NX:
  2877'   2A 2DAF'              	LHLD	SLINES
  287A'   2B                    	DCX	H
  287B'   22 2DAF'              	SHLD	SLINES
  287E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  287F'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2880'   CA 289E'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2883'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2886'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2887'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  288A'   3A 2DAB'              	LDA	SANY
  288D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  288E'   CA 2898'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2891'   2A 2DAD'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2894'   23                    	INX	H
  2895'   22 2DAD'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2898'                         SUB_AE:
  2898'   CD 0A63'              	CALL	ATEOF
  289B'   D2 2865'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  289E'                         SUBDON:
  289E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  28A1'   3A 2DAB'              	LDA	SANY
  28A4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28A5'   CA 28C3'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  28A8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  28AA'   32 2CE6'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  28AD'   2A 2DAD'              	LHLD	SBACK
  28B0'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28B1'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28B2'   CA 28BD'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  28B5'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  28B7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  28BA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  28BD'                         SUB_PL:
  28BD'   CD 1244'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  28C0'   C3 07C1'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  28C3'                         SUBNFD:
  28C3'   21 2DB5'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  28C6'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  28C9'   2A 2DB8'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  28CC'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  28CF'   21 2629'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  28D2'   CD 259F'              	CALL	EXERR
  28D5'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  28D8'   C3 259A'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  28DB'                         SUBBAD:
  28DB'   21 2617'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  28DE'   C3 2594'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  28E1'                         SUBLIN:
  28E1'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28E2'   32 2DAC'              	STA	SLCHG
  28E5'   CD 2960'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  28E8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  28EB'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  28EB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  28EE'   DA 28F6'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  28F1'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  28F3'   C2 28EB'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  28F6'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  28F6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  28F9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28FA'   CD 17D3'              	CALL	CEND
  28FD'   D1                    	POP	D
  28FE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2901'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2902'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2905'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2906'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2907'   FA 2959'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  290A'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  290B'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  290C'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  290F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2912'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  2915'   DA 2959'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2918'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  291B'   EB                    	XCHG
  291C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  291F'   EB                    	XCHG
  2920'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2923'   EB                    	XCHG
  2924'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2925'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2928'   D1                    	POP	D
  2929'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  292A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  292D'   3A 2DAB'              	LDA	SANY
  2930'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2931'   C2 293E'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2934'   3A 2DAC'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2937'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2938'   C2 293E'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  293B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  293E'                         SUBL_C:
  293E'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2941'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2942'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2944'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2947'   11 2DBA'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  294A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  294D'                         SUBL_I:
  294D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  294F'   32 2DAC'              	STA	SLCHG
  2952'   3A 2DAA'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2955'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2956'   C2 28F6'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2959'                         SUBL_D:
  2959'   CD 2960'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  295C'   3A 2DAC'              	LDA	SLCHG
  295F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2960'                         SUBBOL:
  2960'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2963'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  2966'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2969'                         VC_SRCH:
  2969'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  296C'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  296D'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2970'   22 2D9A'              	SHLD	SCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  2973'   C1                    	POP	B
  2974'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2975'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2977'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2979'   CA 297E'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  297C'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  297E'                         VS_DIR:
  297E'   32 2D6F'              	STA	SDIR
  2981'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2984'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2987'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2988'   CD 1EC8'              	CALL	RDLINE
  298B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  298C'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  298D'   3A 2D46'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2990'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2991'   CA 29A6'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2994'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2995'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2997'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  299A'   19                    	DAD	D
  299B'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  299D'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  299E'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  29A1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29A3'   32 2D71'              	STA	SHAVE
  29A6'                         VS_OLD:
  29A6'   3A 2D71'              	LDA	SHAVE
  29A9'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29AA'   CA 29B6'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  29AD'   3A 2D6F'              	LDA	SDIR
  29B0'   32 2D70'              	STA	SDIRE
  29B3'   C3 29DF'              	JMP	SRCHN
  29B6'                         VS_NOP:
  29B6'   21 2659'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  29B9'   C3 259F'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  29BC'                         VC_N:
  29BC'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  29BF'   3A 2D6F'              	LDA	SDIR
  29C2'   C3 29CC'              	JMP	VN_GO
  29C5'                         VCB_N:
  29C5'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  29C8'   3A 2D6F'              	LDA	SDIR
  29CB'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  29CC'                         VN_GO:
  29CC'   32 2D70'              	STA	SDIRE
  29CF'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  29D2'   22 2D9A'              	SHLD	SCNT
  29D5'   3A 2D71'              	LDA	SHAVE
  29D8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29D9'   CA 29B6'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  29DC'   C3 29DF'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  29DF'                         SRCHN:
  29DF'   CD 122C'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  29E2'   CD 06F4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  29E5'                         SN_LP:
  29E5'   CD 2A1A'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  29E8'   DA 2A05'              	JC	SN_BAD
  29EB'   2A 2D9A'              	LHLD	SCNT
  29EE'   2B                    	DCX	H
  29EF'   22 2D9A'              	SHLD	SCNT
  29F2'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  29F3'   B5                    	ORA	L
  29F4'   C2 29E5'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  29F7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  29FA'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  29FD'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29FF'   32 2D7C'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2A02'   C3 1244'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2A05'                         SN_BAD:
  2A05'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A08'   DA 0749'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2A0B'   CD 073C'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2A0E'   21 2629'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2A11'   CD 259F'              	CALL	EXERR
  2A14'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A17'   C3 259A'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2A1A'                         SRCHGO:
  2A1A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A1C'   32 2DA3'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2A1F'   CD 2A35'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2A22'   D0                    	RNC
  2A23'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2A26'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2A27'   3A 2D70'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A2A'   2F                    	CMA
  2A2B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2A2E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A2F'   32 2DA3'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2A32'   C3 2A35'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2A35'                         SRCHW:
  2A35'   3A 2D70'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2A38'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2A3B'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2A3E'   22 2CE3'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2A41'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A42'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2A45'                         SW_LP:
  2A45'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2A48'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2A49'   CD 2A55'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2A4C'   D0                    	RNC
  2A4D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2A50'   D2 2A45'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2A53'   37                    	STC
  2A54'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2A55'                         SRCH1:
  2A55'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2A58'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2A5B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A5C'   FA 2A80'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2A5F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2A62'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2A65'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2A68'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2A69'   3A 2DA3'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2A6C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A6D'   CA 2A76'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2A70'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2A71'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2A72'   37                    	STC
  2A73'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2A74'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2A75'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2A76'                         SR1_FW:
  2A76'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2A79'   D8                    	RC
  2A7A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2A7D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2A80'                         SR1_BK:
  2A80'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2A83'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2A86'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2A89'   D8                    	RC
  2A8A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2A8D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A90'   D8                    	RC
  2A91'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2A94'                         MSGNAM:
  2A94'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2A95'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2A97'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2A9A'   E1                    	POP	H
  2A9B'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2A9C'   23                    	INX	H
  2A9D'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2A9E'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2AA0'   C2 2AAD'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2AA3'   21 26C7'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2AA6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2AA9'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AAA'   C3 2AF7'              	JMP	MN_END
  2AAD'                         MN_HAS:
  2AAD'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2AAF'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2AB2'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AB3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2AB4'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2AB5'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2AB6'   CA 2AC4'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2AB9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2ABA'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2ABC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2ABF'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2AC1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AC4'                         MN_NAM:
  2AC4'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AC5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2AC6'   23                    	INX	H
  2AC7'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2AC9'                         MN_NM:
  2AC9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2ACA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2ACC'   CA 2AD7'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2ACF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AD2'   23                    	INX	H
  2AD3'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2AD4'   C2 2AC9'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2AD7'                         MN_EXT:
  2AD7'   E1                    	POP	H
  2AD8'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2ADB'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2ADC'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2ADD'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2ADF'   CA 2AF7'              	JZ	MN_END
  2AE2'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2AE4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AE7'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2AE9'                         MN_XL:
  2AE9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2AEA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2AEC'   CA 2AF7'              	JZ	MN_END
  2AEF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2AF2'   23                    	INX	H
  2AF3'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2AF4'   C2 2AE9'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2AF7'                         MN_END:
  2AF7'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2AF9'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2AFC'                         MSGFLG:
  2AFC'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2AFE'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AFF'   CA 2B0F'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B02'   3A 2CE6'              	LDA	MODF
  2B05'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B06'   CA 2B0F'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B09'   21 26D1'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2B0C'   CD 2B24'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B0F'                         MF_RO:
  2B0F'   3A 2D5C'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2B12'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B13'   CA 2B1C'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2B16'   21 26DC'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2B19'   CD 2B24'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2B1C'                         MF_NEW:
  2B1C'   3A 2D5A'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2B1F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B20'   C8                    	RZ
  2B21'   21 26E7'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2B24'                         MF_PUT:
  2B24'   78                    	MOV	A,B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B25'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B26'   C2 2B32'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2B29'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2B2B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B2C'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2B2E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B31'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B32'                         MF_STR:
  2B32'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2B35'                         EMITW:
  2B35'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2B36'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2B37'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2B39'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2B3C'   CD 2B5A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B3F'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2B42'   CD 2B5A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B45'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2B48'   CD 2B5A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B4B'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2B4E'   CD 2B5A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2B51'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2B52'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2B54'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B57'   D1                    	POP	D
  2B58'   C1                    	POP	B
  2B59'   C9                    	RET
  2B5A'                         EWDIG:
  2B5A'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2B5C'                         EW_LP:
  2B5C'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2B5D'   93                    	SUB	E
  2B5E'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2B5F'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2B60'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2B61'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2B62'   DA 2B69'              	JC	EW_DN
  2B65'   0C                    	INR	C
  2B66'   C3 2B5C'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2B69'                         EW_DN:
  2B69'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2B6A'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2B6B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B6C'   C2 2B72'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2B6F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2B70'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B71'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2B72'                         EW_PR:
  2B72'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2B73'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2B75'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B78'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B7A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2B7B'                         ISEMPT:
  2B7B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2B7E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2B7F'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2B80'   C0                    	RNZ
  2B81'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2B84'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2B85'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2B86'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2B87'                         MSGCTG:
  2B87'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2B8A'   CD 2A94'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2B8D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2B8F'   CD 2AFC'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2B92'   CD 2B7B'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2B95'   C2 2B9E'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2B98'   21 26FA'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2B9B'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2B9E'                         MG_POS:
  2B9E'   21 26ED'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2BA1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BA4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2BA7'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2BA8'   CD 2B35'              	CALL	EMITW
  2BAB'   21 26F4'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2BAE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2BB1'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2BB4'   23                    	INX	H
  2BB5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BB6'   CD 2B35'              	CALL	EMITW
  2BB9'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2BBC'   23                    	INX	H
  2BBD'   D1                    	POP	D
  2BBE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2BC1'   C8                    	RZ
  2BC2'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BC3'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2BC5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BC8'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BC9'   C3 2B35'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2BCC'                         FILMSG:
  2BCC'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2BCF'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BD1'   C8                    	RZ
  2BD2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2BD5'   CD 2A94'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2BD8'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2BD9'   C3 2AFC'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2BDC'                         WRMSG:
  2BDC'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2BDF'   CD 2A94'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2BE2'   21 270C'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2BE5'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2BE8'                         MSGPST:
  2BE8'   3A 2D59'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2BEB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BEC'   C8                    	RZ
  2BED'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2BEE'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2BEF'   32 2D59'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2BF2'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2BF3'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2BF5'   CA 2C11'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2BF8'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2BFA'   CA 2C17'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2BFD'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2BFF'   CA 2C08'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2C02'   CD 2B87'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2C05'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C08'                         MP_BIG:
  2C08'   21 26A8'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2C0B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2C0E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C11'                         MP_FIL:
  2C11'   CD 2BCC'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2C14'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2C17'                         MP_WRT:
  2C17'   CD 2BDC'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2C1A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2C1D'                         VC_CTLG:
  2C1D'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2C1F'   32 2D59'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C22'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2C24'   32 2CE7'              	STA	RINTENT
  2C27'   C3 06CB'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2C2A'                         CMDTAB:
  2C2A'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C2B'   2C1D'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2C2D'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2C2E'   07F9'                 	DW	VC_H
  2C30'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2C31'   0823'                 	DW	VC_L
  2C33'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2C34'   08F4'                 	DW	VC_J
  2C36'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2C37'   0906'                 	DW	VC_K
  2C39'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2C3A'   0856'                 	DW	VC_0
  2C3C'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2C3D'   0874'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2C3F'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2C40'   08B2'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2C42'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2C43'   08D6'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2C45'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2C46'   0AE5'                 	DW	VC_W
  2C48'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2C49'   0B36'                 	DW	VC_B
  2C4B'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2C4C'   0CCC'                 	DW	VC_E
  2C4E'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2C4F'   0AEC'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2C51'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2C52'   0B3D'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2C54'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2C55'   0CD3'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2C57'   47                    	DB	'G'
  2C58'   116A'                 	DW	VC_G
  2C5A'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2C5B'   11BD'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2C5D'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2C5E'   179C'                 	DW	VC_X
  2C60'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2C61'   1F3B'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2C63'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2C64'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2C66'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2C67'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2C69'   74                    	DB	't'
  2C6A'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2C6C'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2C6D'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2C6F'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2C70'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2C72'   2C                    	DB	','
  2C73'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2C75'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2C76'   1A73'                 	DW	VC_Y
  2C78'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2C79'   1A7B'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2C7B'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2C7C'   1CB1'                 	DW	VC_P
  2C7E'   50                    	DB	'P'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C7F'   1CC6'                 	DW	VCB_P
  2C81'   69                    	DB	'i'
  2C82'   17F4'                 	DW	VC_I
  2C84'   61                    	DB	'a'
  2C85'   17FA'                 	DW	VC_A
  2C87'   41                    	DB	'A'
  2C88'   181A'                 	DW	VCB_A
  2C8A'   49                    	DB	'I'
  2C8B'   1836'                 	DW	VCB_I
  2C8D'   72                    	DB	'r'
  2C8E'   1978'                 	DW	VC_R
  2C90'   52                    	DB	'R'
  2C91'   19CC'                 	DW	VCB_R
  2C93'   64                    	DB	'd'
  2C94'   19E1'                 	DW	VC_D
  2C96'   44                    	DB	'D'
  2C97'   1784'                 	DW	VCB_D
  2C99'   43                    	DB	'C'
  2C9A'   177E'                 	DW	VCB_C
  2C9C'   63                    	DB	'c'
  2C9D'   19E9'                 	DW	VC_C
  2C9F'   6F                    	DB	'o'
  2CA0'   1884'                 	DW	VC_O
  2CA2'   4F                    	DB	'O'
  2CA3'   1899'                 	DW	VCB_O
  2CA5'   4A                    	DB	'J'
  2CA6'   18B3'                 	DW	VCB_J
  2CA8'   7E                    	DB	'~'
  2CA9'   1935'                 	DW	VC_TILD
  2CAB'   3A                    	DB	':'
  2CAC'   1F66'                 	DW	VC_EX
  2CAE'   2F                    	DB	'/'
  2CAF'   2969'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2CB1'   3F                    	DB	'?'
  2CB2'   2969'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2CB4'   6E                    	DB	'n'
  2CB5'   29BC'                 	DW	VC_N
  2CB7'   4E                    	DB	'N'
  2CB8'   29C5'                 	DW	VCB_N
  2CBA'   48                    	DB	'H'
  2CBB'   0D90'                 	DW	VC_HIGH
  2CBD'   4D                    	DB	'M'
  2CBE'   0DA7'                 	DW	VC_MIDL
  2CC0'   4C                    	DB	'L'
  2CC1'   0DCA'                 	DW	VC_LOWL
  2CC3'   06                    	DB	06H			; ^F -- page forward
  2CC4'   0DE7'                 	DW	VC_PGDN
  2CC6'   02                    	DB	02H			; ^B -- page back
  2CC7'   0E45'                 	DW	VC_PGUP
  2CC9'   04                    	DB	04H			; ^D -- half page down
  2CCA'   0F26'                 	DW	VC_HFDN
  2CCC'   15                    	DB	15H			; ^U -- half page up
  2CCD'   0FBF'                 	DW	VC_HFUP
  2CCF'   0C                    	DB	0CH			; ^L -- redraw
  2CD0'   1167'                 	DW	VC_REDR
  2CD2'   2E                    	DB	'.'			; '.' -- repeat the last change
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CD3'   017D'                 	DW	VC_DOT
  2CD5'   75                    	DB	'u'			; 'u' -- undo (and undo the undo)
  2CD6'   0545'                 	DW	VC_UNDO
  2CD8'   6D                    	DB	'm'		; m{a-c} -- set a mark
  2CD9'   032E'                 	DW	VC_MK
  2CDB'   60                    	DB	60H		; `{a-c} -- back to its exact spot
  2CDC'   0333'                 	DW	VC_BQ
  2CDE'   27                    	DB	27H		; '{a-c} -- back to its line's first non-blank
  2CDF'   0338'                 	DW	VC_AQ
  2CE1'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DATA
                                ;============================================================
  2CE2'   00                    EDMODE: DB	EM_CMD
  2CE3'   0000                  CMDCNT: DW	0		; count prefix (0 = none)
  2CE5'   00                    QUITF:	DB	0		; set 1 by :q / :wq
  2CE6'   00                    MODF:	DB	0		; 1 = unsaved changes
  2CE7'   00                    RINTENT:DB	R_FULL		; render intent for the last key (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2CE8'   01                    ILN:	DB	1		; lines an R_ILIN paint opens (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2CE9'   00                    ICHAPP:	DB	0		; 1 = the R_ICH char went at the row's end, so
                                				;   nothing follows it to shift: no ICH needed
  2CEA'   00                    ILABOV:	DB	0		; 1 = the row above an R_ILIN's opened rows lost
                                				;   text to them and must be redrawn too
  2CEB'   FFFE                  GOALCOL:DW	0FFFEH		; sticky goal screen column for j/k (0FFFFH = EOL, 0FFFEH = stale)
  2CED'   00                    WBIG:	DB	0		; word motion: 0 = w/b/e, 1 = W/B/E
  2CEE'   00                    WPULL:	DB	0		; w/W ended off a line's end and was pulled back
  2CEF'   00                    HFAMT:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: rows (vim's 'scroll'); 0 = NEDIT/2
  2CF0'   00                    HFN:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: the rows this one goes
  2CF1'   0000                  HFTP:	DW	0		; ^D/^U: -> the new top line's entry
  2CF3'   00                    HTN:	DB	0		; row table: its entries
  2CF4'   00                    HTCUR:	DB	0		; ... the cursor line's
  2CF5'   00                    HTTP:	DB	0		; ... the top line's
  2CF6'   00                    HTEOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its last is the file's last line
  2CF7'   00                    HTBOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its first is the file's first
  2CF8'   0000                  RWR:	DW	0		; ^F/^B: rows still to go
  2CFA'   00                    RWMV:	DB	0		; ^F/^B: 1 = the table was made again on the way
  2CFB'   00                    OVDIR:	DB	0		; OVLAP: 0 = h2.. are above h1 in the table
  2CFC'   0000                  JKCNT:	DW	0		; j/k: count
  2CFE'   0000                  JKN:	DW	0		; j/k: lines scanned for (min(count, limit))
  2D00'   0000                  JKMOV:	DW	0		; j: lines moved
  2D02'   0000                  JKBK:	DW	0		; k: scan position, bytes back from GAPBEG
  2D04'   00                    JKPHN:	DB	0		; j: 1 = moved may be one less (last LF ends the file)
  2D05'   00                    JKLST:	DB	0		; j: last byte of the scan
  2D06'   0000                  JKVC:	DW	0		; j/k landing: screen column
  2D08'   0000                  JKLS:	DW	0		; j/k landing: last content char (0FFFFH = none)
  0001                          PG	EQU	1		; pending-prefix codes held in GPEND/GSAVE
  0002                          PZ	EQU	2
  0003                          PR	EQU	3		;   ('r', whose next key is its operand)
  0004                          PM	EQU	4		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running (MKWHAT, not
  0005                          PJ	EQU	5		;   GPEND: each reads its own mark letter, so none
  0006                          PJL	EQU	6		;   of them leaves anything pending)
                                ;--- '.' (repeat): see the '.' section above for how these are driven.
  2D0A'   00                    DOTCHG: DB	0		; 1 = the command being typed changed the text
  2D0B'   01                    DOTSTA: DB	1		; 1 = the next key starts a new recording
  2D0C'   00                    DOTRPL: DB	0		; 1 = '.' is replaying, so nothing is recorded
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-139


  2D0D'   00                    DOTOVF: DB	0		; 1 = the change outgrew the scratch
  2D0E'   00                    DOTLK:	DB	0		; the last key recorded, encoded
  2D0F'   0000*                 DOTSP:	DW	DOTSCR		; the scratch's write pointer
  2D11'   00                    DOTLEN: DB	0		; keys in DOTBUF (0 = nothing to repeat)
  2D12'   00                    DOTIX:	DB	0		; the replay's read index into DOTBUF
  2D13'   0000                  DOTNCT: DW	0		; the count typed on the '.' (0 = none)
                                
  2D15'   00                    GPEND:	DB	0		; pending 'g' / 'Z' / 'r' (gg / ZZ / r{char})
  2D16'   00                    GSAVE:	DB	0		; the pending snapshot for the current command
  2D17'   00                    PENDOP: DB	0		; pending operator ('d' for dd)
  2D18'   00                    PSAVE:	DB	0		; pending-op snapshot for the current command
  2D19'   00                    DDSNG:	DB	0		; 1 = the current dd deletes a single line (DL paint)
  2D1A'   00                    OPCLSV: DB	0		; operator: motion class (0 excl, 1 incl, 2 linewise,
                                				;   3 excl but a jump -- no end-of-line clamp)
  2D1B'   00                    CWEOL:	DB	0		; operator: the motion is 'cw' rewritten to 'ce',
                                				;   and its first word is yet to be stepped
  2D1C'   0000                  OPSTA:	DW	0		; operator: span start (cursor before the motion)
  2D1E'   0000                  OPEND2: DW	0		; operator: span end (cursor after the motion)
  2D20'   00 00 00              OPSFP:	DB	0,0,0		; operator: the start as a place in the file
  2D23'   00 00 00              OPSLO:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where its line starts
  2D26'   00 00 00              OPSHI:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where the next line does (OPPGD: the span's
                                				;   high end, whichever line that turned out to be)
  2D29'   00 00 00              OPTOGO: DB	0,0,0		; OPPGD: bytes still to take
  2D2C'   0000                  OPLO:	DW	0		; operator: low offset of the delete range
  2D2E'   0000                  OPHI:	DW	0		; operator: high offset of the delete range
                                
  2D30'   0000                  CNTV:	DW	0		; motion repeat counter
  2D32'   0000                  TTI:	DW	0		; TAILTAB scan index
  2D34'   0000                  WKI:	DW	0		; scan index
  2D36'   0000                  WKN:	DW	0		; cached LOGLEN
  2D38'   0000                  WKTERM: DW	0		; line terminator offset
  2D3A'   0000                  WKBOL:	DW	0		; EOLC scratch: line BOL
  2D3C'   00                    WKCLS:	DB	0		; word motion: class of the current run
  2D3D'   00                    VRCH:	DB	0		; 'r' / R: the char being stored
  2D3E'   0000                  RPDEL:	DW	0		; R: chars overwritten, still in the gap (BS puts back)
  2D40'   0000                  RPAPP:	DW	0		; R: chars appended past the line end (BS deletes)
  2D42'   0000                  RPGE:	DW	0		; R: GAPEND as the last overwrite left it
                                
                                
  2D44'   0000                  RLPTR:	DW	0		; RDLINE: write pointer
  2D46'   00                    RLLEN:	DB	0		; RDLINE: chars stored
  2D47'   00                    RLMAX:	DB	0		; RDLINE: capacity
  2D48'   00                    EXOP:	DB	0		; ex: the command (EO_xx)
  2D49'   00                    EXARG:	DB	0		; ex: 0 = no file name given
  2D4A'   00                    EXBAK:	DB	0		; ex: the write keeps a backup
  2D4B'   0000                  EXCMD:	DW	0		; ex: text shown after a message
  2D4D'   00                    WPROW:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's row
  2D4E'   00                    WPBYT:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's byte in its record
  2D4F'   0000                  WPREC:	DW	0		; :w -- the cursor's record in the file
  2D51'   0000                  ZDIF:	DW	0		; G/gg: target - top (low 16 bits)
  2D53'   00                    ZLF:	DB	0		; G/gg: lines from the target up to the top
  2D54'   0000                  ZSVCNT: DW	0		; G/gg: CMDCNT around LINPSB
  2D56'   0000                  ZSVAUX: DW	0		; G/gg: AUXCNT around LINPSB
  2D58'   00                    ZSVDIR: DB	0		; G/gg: DIRFLG around LINPSB
  2D59'   00                    MSGWHT: DB	0		; the message the bottom row still owes (MW_xxx)
  2D5A'   00                    MSGNEW: DB	0		; the file was not there when it was opened ('[New]')
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-140


  2D5B'   00                    EXBANG: DB	0		; ex: 0 = '!' given
  2D5C'   00                    ROFLAG: DB	0		; command line: '-R' / '/R' was given
  2D5D'   00                    PLUSF:	DB	0		; command line: '+' was given
  2D5E'   00                    YMODF:	DB	0		; yy: MODF across the delete + put back
  2D5F'   00                    YDCHG:	DB	0		; yy: DOTCHG across it too (see '.')
  2D60'   00                    YURPL:	DB	0		; yy: UNDRPL across it too (see 'u')
  2D61'   0000                  YOFF:	DW	0		; yy: the cursor, which a yank leaves alone
  2D63'   0000                  YPT:	DW	0		; yy: where the lines came from
  2D65'   0000                  PCNT:	DW	0		; p/P: copies still to put
  2D67'   00 00 00              PPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; p/P: the first line put, a file position
  2D6A'   0000                  PLFS:	DW	0		; p/P: the lines in the register
  2D6C'   00                    PCPY:	DB	0		; p/P: the copies, kept for the paint
  2D6D'   0000                  YLDLT:	DW	0		; yy: LNDLT across the delete + put back
                                
  2D6F'   00                    SDIR:	DB	0		; the last '/' or '?' direction (FFH = back)
  2D70'   00                    SDIRE:	DB	0		; ... the direction THIS search runs in ('N')
  2D71'   00                    SHAVE:	DB	0		; nonzero once a pattern has been entered
  2D72'   00                    FNDKND: DB	0		; f/F/t/T: which find ';' repeats (0 = none yet)
  2D73'   00                    FNDCH:	DB	0		; ... and the character it looked for
  2D74'   00                    FNDRUN: DB	0		; the find THIS command is running (',' reverses)
  2D75'   00                    FNDSTP: DB	0		; how far along it starts: 2 for a 't' repeat
  2D76'   0000                  FNDN:	DW	0		; occurrences still to count off
  2D78'   0000                  FNDCUR: DW	0		; the cursor it started from
  2D7A'   0000                  FNDLIM: DW	0		; ... and the end of the line it may not pass
  2D7C'   00                    GPKEEP: DB	0		; GPLACE: keep the column (a search) not FNBLNK
  2D7D'   00 00 00              MKVAL:	DB	0,0,0		; marks: nonzero once 'm' has set that one.  It
                                				;   lives HERE and not in MKTAB because the image
                                				;   guarantees a DB, and nothing guarantees a DS
  2D80'   00 00 00              MKEDIT: DB	0,0,0		; MKSHFT: where in the file the edit is
  2D83'   0000                  MKN:	DW	0		; ... how long it is
  2D85'   00                    MKTYP:	DB	0		; ... and which way: 1 text went in, 2 came out
  2D86'   00 00 00              MKPOS:	DB	0,0,0		; MKJMP: the file position being jumped to
  2D89'   00                    MKWHAT: DB	0		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running
  2D8A'   00                    MKHOLD: DB	0		; set across YKGO's delete + put back (a yank
                                				;   that could not copy): MKSHFT must leave the
                                				;   marks alone through it
  2D8B'   0000                  YACT:	DW	0		; how this yank takes its lines (VD_DD / YSPAN)
  2D8D'   0000                  YTGT:	DW	0		; YSPAN: the text's length once the span is out
  2D8F'   0000                  YWAS:	DW	0		; ... and its length BEFORE the lines came out
  2D91'   0000                  YCOL:	DW	0		; ... how far into them the cursor sat
  2D93'   00 00 00              YPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; ... and where in the file they start
                                ;--- the yank that copies instead (YNCOPY / YNSPAN) ---
  2D96'   0000                  YNPG:	DW	0		; the window the yank started in (YNDONE's paint)
  2D98'   0000                  YNCNT:	DW	0		; YNLINE: the lines it still wants
  2D9A'   0000                  SCNT:	DW	0		; searches still to do (the count)
  2D9C'   00 00 00              SOFF:	DB	0,0,0		; BRKON: where in the FILE the command began
  2D9F'   0000                  SLDLT:	DW	0		; ... and LNDLT: for a search that fails, and
                                				;   any move an ESC stops
  2DA1'   00                    BRKARM: DB	0		; nonzero: CNTTST looks for an ESC
  2DA2'   00                    BRKHIT: DB	0		; nonzero: one was found, and the count spent
  2DA3'   00                    SSKIP:	DB	0		; skip the character under the cursor (not
                                				;   on the wrapped pass)
                                
  0024                          SREPMX	EQU	EXMAX-4		; ':s' -- bounded by the ex line itself, so
                                				;   SCOPY can never truncate a line anyone is
                                				;   able to type: the shortest possible
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-141


                                				;   substitute is 's' '/' + one pattern char +
                                				;   '/' before the replacement starts, and
                                				;   RDLINE stores at most EXMAX.  Tied to
                                				;   EXMAX rather than written out, the way
                                				;   BUF.MAC ties SRCMAX to SRMAX, so the two
                                				;   cannot drift apart.
  2DA4'   00                    SRNGF:	DB	0		; ex: a line range was given
  2DA5'   0000                  SRNG1:	DW	0		; ex: its first line
  2DA7'   0000                  SRNG2:	DW	0		; ... and its last (FFFFH = the file's last)
  2DA9'   00                    SDELIM: DB	0		; ':s' the delimiter typed
  2DAA'   00                    SGFLAG: DB	0		; ':s' the 'g' flag
  2DAB'   00                    SANY:	DB	0		; ':s' something in the range has changed
  2DAC'   00                    SLCHG:	DB	0		; ':s' this line changed
  2DAD'   0000                  SBACK:	DW	0		; ':s' lines walked past the last change
  2DAF'   0000                  SLINES: DW	0		; ':s' / ':N,Mw' lines of the range still to do
  2DB1'   00                    RWPTR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' the next free byte of the record at
                                				;   0080H, as that address's low byte
  2DB2'   00                    RWERR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' a record would not go: the disk is full
  2DB3'   00                    RDANY:	DB	0		; ':r' something has been read in
  2DB4'   00                    RDBRK:	DB	0		; ':r' the line above wants a line end first
  2DB5'   00 00 00              SOFFS:	DB	0,0,0		; ':s' the cursor's place in the file, for one that
  2DB8'   0000                  SLDLTS: DW	0		;   changes nothing, and its LNDLT
  2DBA'   00                    SREPLN: DB	0		; ':s' the replacement's length, kept
  2DBB'                         SREPL:	DS	SREPMX+1	;   just below it: WM's RDSTR layout,
                                				;   so INSSTR can splice it
                                
                                	END
